## Test Anti-Pattern Audit: `ProductCatalogTests.cs`

**Framework:** MSTest · **Tests:** 10 · **Production methods covered:** 10/10

### Summary

| Severity | Count |
|----------|-------|
| 🔴 Critical | 2 (systemic — affects all 10 tests) |
| 🟡 Medium | 1 |
| Total | 3 |

This is a textbook **coverage-touching** test class. Every public method on `ProductCatalog` is called, achieving high line coverage, but **6 of 10 tests have zero assertions** and the remaining 4 only assert `IsNotNull` — a check that can never fail for the types returned here (`List<T>` and `string` are never null from these methods). **None of these tests can catch a regression.**

---

### 🔴 Critical #1 — Coverage Touching (systemic, all 10 tests)

The class methodically calls every public member in near-declaration order with no meaningful verification. This is the exact pattern that produces 95% coverage and 0% defect detection.

**6 tests with zero assertions:**

| Test (line) | What it misses |
|---|---|
| `AddProduct_Works` (L17) | Never verifies the product was persisted |
| `UpdatePrice_Works` (L33) | Never verifies the price actually changed |
| `RemoveProduct_Works` (L39) | Never verifies the product is gone |
| `GetProductCount_Works` (L64) | Discards the return value entirely |
| `ApplyBulkDiscount_Works` (L69) | Never verifies prices were reduced |
| `ImportFromCsv_Works` (L85) | Never verifies products were imported |

**4 tests with only `Assert.IsNotNull`:**

| Test (line) | Why `IsNotNull` is meaningless here |
|---|---|
| `GetProduct_Works` (L28) | `FindBySku` returns a `Product` — never checks Sku/Name/Price |
| `SearchProducts_Works` (L51) | `Search` returns `List<Product>` (never null) — never checks contents or count |
| `GetAllProducts_Works` (L59) | `GetAll` returns `List<Product>` (never null) — never checks count |
| `ExportToCsv_Works` (L82) | `ExportToCsv` returns `string.Join(...)` (never null) — never checks CSV content |

**Concrete fixes (before → after):**

```csharp
// AddProduct_Works → AddProduct_PersistsProduct
[TestMethod]
public void AddProduct_PersistsProduct()
{
    var product = new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m);
    _catalog.AddProduct(product);

